WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.240
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:10.190
In this piece of work, we are interested in
creating a 3D virtual tour for an apartment.

00:00:10.190 --> 00:00:15.700
However, for this apartment, no 3D information
is available - instead, the input for the

00:00:15.700 --> 00:00:21.300
algorithm is something that we can obtain
easily, in this case, a 2D floorplan and a

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:24.290
set of images that we shot in the apartment.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:29.070
From this information, we would create a 3D
floorplan that is not only faithful to the

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:34.289
real one in terms of geometry, but the photos
with the correct viewpoints are also to be

00:00:34.289 --> 00:00:36.629
assigned to the correct walls.

00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:41.579
In order to accomplish this, one has to overcome
a series of challenging problems.

00:00:41.579 --> 00:00:46.109
For instance, we have to estimate the layout
of each room and find the location of the

00:00:46.109 --> 00:00:48.300
camera in each of these images.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:53.620
Also, to obtain high-quality solutions, the
goal is to extract as much information from

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:55.319
the inputs as possible.

00:00:55.319 --> 00:00:59.889
The authors recognized that the floorplans
provide way more information than we take

00:00:59.889 --> 00:01:01.010
for granted.

00:01:01.010 --> 00:01:05.800
For instance, beyond showing the geometric
relation of the rooms, it can also be used

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:10.040
to find out the aspect ratios of the floor
for each room.

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:16.210
The window-to-wall ratios can also be approximated
and matched between the photos and the floorplan.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:20.800
This additional information is super useful
when trying to find out which room is to be

00:01:20.800 --> 00:01:24.420
assigned to which part of the 3D floorplan.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:29.530
Beyond just looking at the photos, we also
have access to a large swath of learning algorithms

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:34.360
that can reliably classify whether we're looking
at a bathroom or a living room.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:38.950
There are even more constraints to adhere
to in order to aggressively reduce the number

00:01:38.950 --> 00:01:43.250
of physical configurations, make sure to have
a look at the paper for details, there are

00:01:43.250 --> 00:01:45.430
lots of cool tricks described there.

00:01:45.430 --> 00:01:48.110
As always, there is a link to it in the video
description.

00:01:48.110 --> 00:01:53.310
For instance, since the space of possible
solutions is still too vast, a branch and

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:58.000
bound type algorithm is proposed to further
decimate the number of potential solutions

00:01:58.000 --> 00:01:59.200
to evaluate.

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:04.110
And as you can see here, the comparisons against
ground truth floorplans reveal that these

00:02:04.110 --> 00:02:07.530
solutions are indeed quite faithful to reality.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:13.620
The authors also kindly provided a dataset
with more than 200 full apartments with well

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:19.140
over a thousand photos and annotations for
future use in followup research works.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:23.280
Creating such a dataset and publishing it
is incredibly laborious, and could easily

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:28.130
be a paper on its own, and here, we also get
an excellent solution for this problem as

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.130
well.

00:02:29.130 --> 00:02:33.600
In a separate work, the authors also published
a different version of this problem formulation

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:37.700
that reconstructs the exteriors of buildings
in a similar manner.

00:02:37.700 --> 00:02:42.140
There is so much to explore, the links are
available in the video description, make sure

00:02:42.140 --> 00:02:43.140
to have a look!

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.520
In case you're wondering, it's still considered
a crime not doing that.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:51.820
I hope you have enjoyed this episode, and
I find it so delightful to see this unbelievably

00:02:51.820 --> 00:02:53.510
rapid growth on the channel.

00:02:53.510 --> 00:03:00.580
Earlier I thought that even 2 would be amazing,
but now, we have exactly 8 times as many subscribers

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:01.950
as one year ago.

00:03:01.950 --> 00:03:07.620
Words fail me to describe the joy of showing
these amazing works to such a rapidly growing

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:08.620
audience.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.550
This is why I always say at the end of every
episode...

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:15.120
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

